WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.720
It never reached the same heights as the likes&nbsp;
of "Star Trek" and "Star Wars," but "Stargate"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.840
still managed to carve out a place for itself&nbsp;
in the sci-fi genre. Where is the cast today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.800
and how much have they changed over&nbsp;
the decades? Let's take a look.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.520
Playing the part of hyper-intelligent&nbsp;
Egyptologist Dr. Daniel Jackson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.760
James Spader originated the role that was&nbsp;
taken up by Michael Shanks on "Stargate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.920
SG-1". It's Spader's character that&nbsp;
unlocks the secrets of the Stargate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.360
deciphering some hieroglyphics that turn out to be&nbsp;
coordinates based on the constellations. He agrees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:36.320
to accompany Kurt Russell's Colonel Jack O'Neill&nbsp;
and his men through the wormhole he helped open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.760
stepping onto an arid planet ruled by the&nbsp;
Egyptian god Ra, who is actually an ancient alien.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.920
Spader broke out almost a decade before&nbsp;
"Stargate," putting in a scene-stealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:50.880
performance as elitist bully Steff in John&nbsp;
Hughes' classic "Pretty in Pink." He was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.680
danger of being typecast as a silver-spoon brat&nbsp;
in the '80s, but he proved his versatility the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:01.360
following decade. After "Stargate," Spader starred&nbsp;
in David Cronenberg's kinky thriller "Crash," and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.200
more recently, he voiced the titular&nbsp;
villain in "Avengers: Age of Ultron."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.680
Nowadays, he's best known for his TV work,&nbsp;
having played Raymond "Red" Reddington on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.920
NBC's "The Blacklist" since 2013. The show got&nbsp;
delayed during the coronavirus pandemic in 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.960
which allowed him to spend some&nbsp;
quality time with his youngest son.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.960
"We'd go out and line up beer cans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.360
on the big rock out on the far end of the&nbsp;
garden and shoot at them with BB guns."

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.400
The character Ra has been featured in numerous&nbsp;
pieces of "Stargate" media over the years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.080
The androgynous Egyptian god showed up in episodes&nbsp;
of "Stargate SG-1" and "Stargate Origins,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.440
and also played a part in&nbsp;
direct-to-DVD movie "Stargate:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.920
Continuum." Ra's most iconic appearance&nbsp;
is in the first "Stargate" movie, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.480
and that's largely down to the mesmerizing&nbsp;
Jaye Davidson, a dressmaker who got close to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.880
tasting Oscar glory after being spotted&nbsp;
by a film scout in a bar one night.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.760
Despite not being an actor by trade, Davidson&nbsp;
was cast as the transgender character Dil in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.880
1992's "The Crying Game" two years before&nbsp;
his involvement in "Stargate". The film was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.760
nominated for Best Picture the following year,&nbsp;
and Davidson was up for Best Supporting Actor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:14.960
The Oscar buzz made Davidson an overnight star,&nbsp;
and the pressure quickly became too much for him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.680
He decided to quit acting, but when the part of&nbsp;
Ra came along and the studio agreed to pay him a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.440
million dollars, he gave it another go. Davidson&nbsp;
turned in yet another terrific performance as Ra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.160
in what would be his last major appearance.&nbsp;
After appearing in the movie "Jiggery Pokery",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.640
he promptly returned to his first love: fashion.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.240
Indian-American character actor Erick Avari has&nbsp;
one of those faces that you've definitely seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:43.200
before, though you're not entirely sure where.&nbsp;
In "Stargate," he plays village elder Kasuf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.240
who welcomes the explorers warmly when they&nbsp;
arrive. He's particularly fond of James Spader's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.520
Dr. Jackson, who unknowingly agrees to take his&nbsp;
daughter's hand in marriage. Avari is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.680
few who went on to reprise his "Stargate" role,&nbsp;
playing Kasuf in three seasons of "Stargate SG-1".

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:04.480
Avari stuck with "Stargate" director Roland&nbsp;
Emmerich after making his debut as Kasuf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:09.600
following him onto his next big sci-fi epic,&nbsp;
"Independence Day," in which he popped up briefly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.640
as a scientist. He landed a far more substantial&nbsp;
role in the Brendan Fraser-led action adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.240
"The Mummy" a few years later, appearing as Dr.&nbsp;
Terrence Bey, curator of the Cairo Museum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.760
Antiquities and boss of Rachel Weisz's character&nbsp;
Evelyn Carnahan. He also played an ill-fated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.560
human in Tim Burton's "Planet of the Apes,"&nbsp;
a lawyer in Adam Sandler comedy "Mr. Deeds,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.080
and a hot dog cart vendor in Richard Gere's&nbsp;
"Hachi: A Dog's Tale," among many other roles.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:41.360
Video game fans will no doubt recognize&nbsp;
Avari's distinctive tones from 2014's "Destiny"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.800
and 2017's "Destiny 2," in which he voiced&nbsp;
Master Rahool. In his most recent role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.400
he played mysterious biblical&nbsp;
figure Nicodemus in "The Chosen,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.760
a crowdfunded TV series about&nbsp;
the life of Jesus Christ.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.200
Kasuf's daughter Sha'uri — the one who gets&nbsp;
offered up to Dr. Daniel Jackson — was played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.880
Israeli actor Mili Avital. The unlikely pair fall&nbsp;
for each other as they battle Ra, and when his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.920
rule comes to an end, Jackson opts to stay behind&nbsp;
on the newly liberated planet. If you cut out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.200
part where Sha'uri is basically given away as a&nbsp;
gift, it's a fairytale ending done sci-fi style.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.880
Avital was a newcomer in Hollywood&nbsp;
when she scored her "Stargate" role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.720
It was just her third appearance on the&nbsp;
big screen, and, despite the mixed reviews,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:27.680
the film opened doors for her. She worked&nbsp;
tirelessly in the latter part of the 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:33.200
appearing in no less than nine movies between&nbsp;
1995 and 2000. Her most notable credits from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.000
the time include the Jim Jarmusch Western "Dead&nbsp;
Man," in which she played a former sex worker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.880
and the rom-com "Kissing a Fool," in which she has&nbsp;
to choose between David Schwimmer and Jason Lee.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:48.160
She spent time in Morocco and Turkey working&nbsp;
on ABC's lavish miniseries "Arabian Nights,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.200
then returned to Israel to continue her career at&nbsp;
home. She still pops up on American screens every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.480
now and again, however, most recently in a 2015&nbsp;
episode of "Law &amp; Order: Special Victims Unit."

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.640
Alexis Cruz plays Skaara, Kasuf's son. He is&nbsp;
heavily involved in the uprising against Ra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.560
but his most memorable scenes are the ones&nbsp;
shared with Kurt Russell's Captain O'Neill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.400
who is still grieving the loss of his&nbsp;
child when we enter the "Stargate" story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.680
Spending time with Skaara — who, in&nbsp;
turn, sees the captain as something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.880
of a father figure — helps him do&nbsp;
some healing while on the mission.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:25.120
Cruz made his movie debut in 1987's "The Pick-up&nbsp;
Artist," a rom-com starring Robert Downey Jr. and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.680
Molly Ringwald. "Stargate" was the biggest&nbsp;
opportunity of his then-fledgling career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.840
and his time in the franchise remains the&nbsp;
thing he's best known for. He has, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.720
proven himself to be a versatile performer over&nbsp;
the years. He's appeared in movies like "Why Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.440
Fools Fall in Love" and Sam Raimi's "Drag Me&nbsp;
to Hell," and he's plied his trade on a number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:49.360
of big TV shows, including "ER" and "American&nbsp;
Family." He's been busy in recent years, both in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:55.200
his personal and professional life — he welcomed a&nbsp;
son in 2019, returned to TV in an episode of Anna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:02.320
Kendrick's HBO series "Love Life" in 2020, and,&nbsp;
in 2021, he guest starred on CBS procedural "FBI."

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:07.120
Benin born model and actor Djimon&nbsp;
Hounsou is pretty well-known these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.080
but back when he appeared as Ra's guard Horus&nbsp;
in "Stargate," he was still relatively unknown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:16.560
He began his career in music videos, acting&nbsp;
in Tina Turner's "I Don't Wanna Lose You,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:20.240
En Vogue's "Hold On," and Janet Jackson's&nbsp;
"Love Will Never Do (Without You)."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:24.720
"Stargate" was his first feature film, and&nbsp;
while it wasn't the one that made him a star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.560
Hounsou didn't have to wait very long&nbsp;
afterwards to break into the mainstream.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:33.840
The West African newcomer was in three films&nbsp;
in 1997, one of which earned him a Golden Globe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.880
nod. He didn't get nominated for an Oscar for&nbsp;
his turn as slave revolt leader Joseph Cinque&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.040
in Steven Spielberg's "Amistad," but&nbsp;
the historical drama put him firmly on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.280
Hollywood's radar. Hounsou would receive&nbsp;
his arguably overdue recognition from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:53.440
Academy for later appearances in 2002's&nbsp;
"In America" and 2006's "Blood Diamond,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.360
being considered in the Best Supporting&nbsp;
Actor category for both pictures.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.560
"You are a good boy, who loves soccer and school."

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.880
In recent years, Hounsou has become a regular&nbsp;
in the superhero genre, working on numerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.360
Marvel projects. After voicing T'Challa in&nbsp;
the animated "Black Panther" miniseries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:16.320
he appeared as Korath in "Guardians of the Galaxy"&nbsp;
and "Captain Marvel," a role he'll reprise in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:21.520
animated Disney+ series "What If...?" He's&nbsp;
got some DC characters on his resume, too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:26.720
Hounsou played Midnight in "Constantine," King&nbsp;
Ricou in "Aquaman," and the Wizard in "Shazam!"

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.800
Character actor Leon Rippy played the role of&nbsp;
commanding officer Lieutenant General W. O. West,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:37.440
the man in charge when Dr. Jackson discovers&nbsp;
how to operate the ancient Stargate portal.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.200
The South Carolina native was already a&nbsp;
favorite of Emmerich's when the filmmaker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:46.640
started casting for "Stargate," having appeared&nbsp;
in his previous films "Moon 44" and "Universal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.880
Soldier." He would go on to appear in three&nbsp;
more of Emmerich's movies: "The Patriot,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:55.360
"The Thirteenth Floor", and the wacky monster&nbsp;
horror extravaganza "Eight Legged Freaks".

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.520
Rippy has only appeared in a handful of&nbsp;
movies since then, the most notable being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:04.480
Gore Verbinski's "The Lone Ranger". He is better&nbsp;
known for his TV work today, having recurred on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:09.280
shows like "Deadwood," "Saving Grace", and the&nbsp;
Stephen King adaptation "Under the Dome." He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.800
reunited with "Stargate" co-star James Spader when&nbsp;
he joined the cast of "The Blacklist" in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.280
and, in a more recent credit, reprised his&nbsp;
"Deadwood" role in a TV movie of the same name.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.120
He would get famous playing the idiotic Harry&nbsp;
Solomon on "3rd Rock from the Sun," but funnyman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.080
French Stewart had a pivotal role in "Stargate"&nbsp;
before he was a sitcom star. The actor made his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.960
feature film debut in Roland Emmerich's sci-fi&nbsp;
adventure, playing Lieutenant Ferretti. He's left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:39.040
in charge of the second unit when the soldiers&nbsp;
arrive, though he's forced to retreat into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.400
temple of Ra during a massive sandstorm, which,&nbsp;
of course, turns out to be a big mistake. The men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.760
under Ferretti's command are taken down by Ra's&nbsp;
guards one by one, before he himself is captured.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:53.840
Stewart made his debut as Harry Solomon&nbsp;
on "3rd Rock from the Sun" in 1996&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:09:00.320
and would remain on the show until it wrapped in&nbsp;
2001. He went on to become a dependable TV actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.760
appearing in the likes of "That '70s Show,"&nbsp;
"Ally McBeal," "Bones," "MadTV," "Community,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:10.400
and even "Stargate Universe," in which he&nbsp;
played a new character, Dr. Andrew Covel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:15.280
He went on to play Principal Cameron on ABC's&nbsp;
"The Middle," but in recent years he's become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:20.880
known for the CBS sitcom "Mom," appearing as&nbsp;
Chef Rudy in dozens of episodes. He's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:26.240
in Michael Lembeck's retirement home comedy&nbsp;
"Queen Bees," scheduled for release in 2021.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:31.920
It wouldn't be sci-fi without some members of the&nbsp;
exploration party being picked off by the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.360
and that was the fate of Derek Webster's&nbsp;
Lieutenant Brown in "Stargate."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.720
He's with Jackson and O'Neill when&nbsp;
they make contact with the locals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.120
and he even gets to sample some local food&nbsp;
before he's killed by one of Ra's guards.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:48.560
Webster made his debut playing a bodyguard in&nbsp;
1981's "Enter the Ninja," a low-budget martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:53.440
arts flick. He then appeared in "Star Trek: The&nbsp;
Next Generation" a year or so before "Stargate,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:58.560
showing up in a 1993 episode as Lieutenant&nbsp;
Sanders. He became a go-to character actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:02.240
for Roland Emmerich after "Stargate,"&nbsp;
joining him on "Independence Day" to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.720
play the pilot of the helicopter that gets&nbsp;
blown up during Operation Welcome Wagon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.760
mankind's ill-fated attempt at communicating with&nbsp;
the alien invaders. After this role, he joined up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.200
with Emmerich again for "Godzilla" in which he&nbsp;
captains the nuclear-powered submarine USS Utah.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:20.800
He established himself as a&nbsp;
solid TV actor in the 2000s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.600
with roles in the likes of "Nip/Tuck," and&nbsp;
"CSI: Miami," and he's still active today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.680
Webster played DA Jackson Holt on "Ray&nbsp;
Donovan" between 2016 and 2017, and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:36.720
2020 he returned to the "Star Trek" franchise&nbsp;
as a Romulan guard in "Star Trek: Picard."

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:42.000
As Colonel Jack O'Neill's right hand man,&nbsp;
John Diehl's Lieutenant Kawalsky helps keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.000
the team together when they are ambushed&nbsp;
by Ra's guards and taken captive aboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:50.720
his ship. Diehl was an experienced character&nbsp;
actor long before he appeared in "Stargate,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:55.680
having established himself in the early 1980s.&nbsp;
He actually shared the screen with Kurt Russell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:59.520
in his second movie appearance, playing&nbsp;
a punk in John Carpenter's "Escape From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.720
New York." He went on to recur as Detective&nbsp;
Larry Zito on "Miami Vice" and appear in Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:08.960
Schumacher's "The Client" before Roland&nbsp;
Emmerich cast him in his sci-fi epic.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:12.080
Diehl has been seen in numerous&nbsp;
feature films since "Stargate,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:16.000
usually in a smaller or supporting role.&nbsp;
He's worked on everything from "Jurassic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.880
Park III" to Michael Bay's "Pearl Harbor".&nbsp;
In recent years, he's taken to old-guy roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:24.800
growing out his grey beard for the likes&nbsp;
of the ranch-set Western "Out of the Wild",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:29.280
in which he played an alcoholic cowboy looking&nbsp;
for redemption. His other notable television&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:33.440
credits include "Snowfall", "Castlerock",&nbsp;
and "The Shield", among many others.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.000
The actor that plays the lead role of Colonel&nbsp;
Jack O'Neill in "Stargate" really needs no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:42.720
introduction. Massachusetts native Kurt&nbsp;
Russell began his career as a child actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:48.720
signing a contract with Disney after impressing&nbsp;
with his performance in 1966's "Follow Me, Boys!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.960
Walt Disney reportedly dealt with the contract&nbsp;
personally and would remain a big fan of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:57.920
young star up until Disney's death. He was already&nbsp;
well known when he took on the role of special ops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:02.400
expert Jack O'Neill years later, having fronted&nbsp;
the likes of John Carpenter's "Escape From&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:07.040
New York," "Big Trouble in Little China," and&nbsp;
"Tombstone," in which he portrayed Wyatt Earp.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.360
After "Stargate", Russell continued to establish&nbsp;
himself as one of Hollywood's most reliable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.080
actors. He experienced what the Toronto Sun&nbsp;
described as, quote, "a spectacular career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:21.280
revival" following his turn as a brutal bounty&nbsp;
hunter in Quentin Tarantino's "The Hateful Eight"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.640
in 2015. He subsequently appeared in a number&nbsp;
of big films, most notably "Guardians of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:31.440
Galaxy Vol. 2" in which he played Star-Lord's&nbsp;
villainous father Ego the Living Planet. More&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.240
recently, Russell has starred as Santa Claus in&nbsp;
Netflix's popular "Christmas Chronicles" movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.920
and he reunited with Tarantino for&nbsp;
"Once Upon a Time... In Hollywood,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:42.400
playing stunt coordinator Randy Miller.

